---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-mailchimp';
const title = 'Drip vs Mailchimp for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Drip and Mailchimp for Shopify campaigns: eligible SMS, CRM and workflow limits, permission, drop calendars, purchase exits and costs.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When does Drip fit?",
    "answer": "Drip can fit an email-led Shopify team seeking coordinated ecommerce workflows, provided its account qualifies for staged US SMS access. Confirm approval, SAP eligibility and email-linked phone identity before relying on SMS."
  },
  {
    "question": "When does Mailchimp fit?",
    "answer": "Mailchimp can fit an established audience CRM and ecommerce email operation. SMS requires an eligible paid marketing plan, supported market, approved application and credits; confirm workflow limits on the purchased tier."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a synced phone number authorize marketing?",
    "answer": "No. Keep email, promotional SMS and transactional purposes separate. Preserve capture source, timestamp, disclosure and revocation evidence; an order or imported phone number alone is not promotional permission."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should purchase exits be tested?",
    "answer": "Place a test order while a promotional message is queued. Verify the matched customer exits before dispatch, then replay the event and confirm no duplicate entry or send. Record event delay and unresolved identities."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should Sequenzy own lifecycle email?",
    "answer": "Only if it is the deliberately chosen lifecycle sender. Drip and Mailchimp already provide email capabilities. Assign each flow one owner and reconcile permissions and exclusions before adding or migrating another tool."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Use account quotes for contact growth, peak sends, SMS segments and credits, add-ons and migration work. Compare incremental contribution per eligible customer using a comparable holdout; do not count discounts or returns twice."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Drip vs Mailchimp</nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Campaign marketing comparison</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs Mailchimp for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Choose around the campaign team, eligible sending scope and reliable Shopify events. Both products offer email and SMS capabilities, with different access conditions; neither gets a blanket win for drops or lifecycle work.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox title="Conditional fits" picks={[
          { label: 'Drip', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Ecommerce workflows with email and eligible SMS after account approval.' },
          { label: 'Mailchimp', tool: 'Mailchimp', reason: 'Audience CRM and campaign operations with eligible paid SMS and verified automation capacity.' },
        ]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Current scope to verify</h2>
        <p>Documentation checked November 25, 2026. <a href="https://www.drip.com/sms">Drip SMS access</a> is staged for existing US paid customers with Support All People enabled. A phone identity requires an email; SMS-only subscribers are unsupported. Request approval before treating the channel as available. Its <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/48924286539661-Quick-Glance-SMS-with-Drip">current overview</a> documents campaigns, workflow SMS and MMS. Older launch descriptions may differ; verify needed message types and sender capabilities in your account.</p>
        <p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/about-sms-marketing/">Mailchimp SMS</a> requires an eligible paid marketing plan, supported sending market, application approval and credits. Audience CRM does not imply a sales pipeline package or unlimited automation. Check <a href="https://mailchimp.com/pricing/marketing/compare-plans/">plan limits</a> for contacts, sends, automation flows, branching and required actions. Drip can suit a team already modeling ecommerce behavior in workflows; Mailchimp can suit a team already managing audiences and campaign calendars there. Rebuild a representative journey in the purchased account before choosing.</p>
        <p>The <a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/connect-shopify/">Mailchimp Shopify connection</a> connects a store to an audience. Review its <a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/mailchimp-shopify-syncing/">sync behavior</a> for customer, product, order and activity data. Do not assume subscription status, browsing events, historical orders or custom fields arrive with the timing and permission your flow needs. Inspect real payloads and integration settings in both products.</p>
        <h2>Identity, permission and event ownership</h2>
        <p>Map Shopify customer and order IDs to the destination profile, email and normalized phone. Quarantine ambiguous merges and shared numbers. Store source, timestamp, disclosure, brand, channel and purpose for permission; a synchronized record or purchase is not marketing consent. Keep promotional SMS, email and transactional service messages separately governed. Preserve revocations with their scope, apply them before dispatch and prevent older imports from restoring permission.</p>
        <p>Assign an owner for capture, campaign approval, integration failures and suppression reconciliation. Test quiet-hour and recipient-timezone behavior, unknown locations, opt-outs and reply handling against the actual supported markets and sender. Review <a href="/guides/consent">consent controls</a> and <a href="/guides/tcpa">applicable requirements</a> with qualified guidance; software settings alone do not settle compliance.</p>
        <p>For purchase exits, queue a cart or drop reminder, place an order and check that the matched customer exits before dispatch. Include delayed and out-of-order order updates, cancellations, guest checkout and duplicate webhook delivery. Use stable event IDs to prevent replay from restarting a flow. Reconcile Shopify orders, destination profiles, queued messages and delivery logs; pause affected sends when identity or suppression cannot be resolved. Record measured latency rather than promising instant synchronization.</p>
        <h2>Proposed merchant workflow tests</h2>
        <p>The following are planning scenarios retained from the original page, not verified merchant case studies or executed results. Set thresholds from inventory, permissions and an agreed measurement design. Run each only on the enabled channels.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Driftwood Surf Co:</strong> coordinate summer boardshort drops with swell-forecast education; compare a permissioned restock waitlist with a broader eligible audience.</li>
          <li><strong>Vinyl Vault Records:</strong> disclose pressing quantity and test priority for waitlisted repeat collectors and high-value buyers without treating lifetime value as consent.</li>
          <li><strong>Bloom &amp; Wick:</strong> plan holiday teasers and shipping deadlines early; separate gift-bundle messages from self-purchase scent education.</li>
          <li><strong>Peak Trail Gear:</strong> isolate bargain-sensitive clearance buyers from full-price loyalists; use a holdout to evaluate discount contribution.</li>
          <li><strong>Sauce Lab:</strong> tie batch-number scarcity messages to reconciled stock and test restrained launch and low-stock reminders with purchase exits.</li>
          <li><strong>Ember Ceramics:</strong> preserve waitlist priority for a small kiln release; use email for the maker story and eligible SMS for access timing without promising unavailable inventory.</li>
          <li><strong>Ridge Run Coffee:</strong> exclude active subscribers from flash offers; test subscription-status delays and campaign collisions.</li>
          <li><strong>Cipher Audio:</strong> sequence a product-story teaser before launch timing for eligible engaged browsers; compare a no-discount offer with a properly measured incentive alternative.</li>
          <li><strong>Patchwork Denim:</strong> match one-of-one listing drops to style preferences; stop queued messages when the relevant item sells.</li>
          <li><strong>Arena Energy:</strong> coordinate athlete-collaboration flavor timing for the gym-bag segment; keep an email gift offer distinct from the SMS timing message.</li>
          <li><strong>Loom &amp; Frame:</strong> disclose edition size and test a sell-through trigger for repeat collectors, verifying live inventory before sending.</li>
          <li><strong>Noir Collective:</strong> coordinate recurring drops and VIP early access; test lower broadcast frequency against an eligible holdout rather than asserting a revenue lift.</li>
          <li><strong>SoleDrop ATX:</strong> preserve size-specific waitlist order, send VIP access first and release general email only when sell-through and remaining sizes justify it.</li>
          <li><strong>Festival Threads Co:</strong> align city and venue-timezone tour campaigns with a per-city frequency policy, including travelers and unknown locations.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Calendar, ownership and migration</h2>
        <p>One calendar should account for launch, scarcity, restock, welcome and replenishment messages across tools. Decide which system enforces frequency, VIP priority and purchase exclusions. If Sequenzy or another email sender is introduced, give each lifecycle flow one sending owner; Drip or Mailchimp may already cover that work. Do not activate duplicate welcome or winback journeys during migration.</p>
        <p>Export permission evidence and exclusions before a switch. Reconcile counts by channel and purpose, investigate mismatches, then test imports and event replay with sending disabled. Define rollback ownership and the system that remains authoritative for new opt-outs. A successful import count alone does not prove usable permission or safe queue behavior.</p>
        <h2>Campaign economics and the decision</h2>
        <p>Request account-specific quotes for current and growing contact counts, peak email sends, SMS subscribers, encoded message segments, credits, add-ons and setup. Verify what each quote includes before adding carrier or sender fees separately. Encoding, images and message length affect billable usage; a campaign is not necessarily one billable unit per recipient. See <a href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a>.</p>
        <p>Choose an evaluation window that covers the purchase cycle and expected order volume, with a comparable eligible-customer holdout. Start with net sales after discounts and returns, subtract product and fulfillment costs, incremental platform and messaging costs, and support costs once. Compare contribution per eligible customer between groups; attributed revenue per send alone does not establish lift. Include migration effort and inventory constraints in the decision.</p>
        <p>Choose Drip when its ecommerce workflow model and approved channel scope fit the team. Choose Mailchimp when its audience operations, purchased automation capacity and eligible SMS meet the same tested requirements. Keep the current sender until identity, permission, purchase exits and reconciliation pass; revisit the choice when the calendar or sending markets change.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs Mailchimp FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
